    "lem-xd-finite-matrix-exponential-series": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-induced-matrix-p-norm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m,n\\in\\mathbb{N}$, let $p\\in\\mathbb{Q}$ with $p\\ge 1$, and let\n$A=(a_{ij})\\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb{R})$ be a real matrix\n([[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]), regarded as the linear map\n$x\\mapsto Ax$ from $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ to $\\mathbb{R}^{m}$ with the published\n$p$-norms ([[def-p-norms-on-rn]]). The **matrix $p$-norm induced by**\n$\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{p}$ is",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-induced-matrix-norms-are-compatible-submultiplicative-and-normalized",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p\\in\\mathbb{Q}$ with $p\\ge 1$ and let $m,n,q\\in\\mathbb{N}$, with the\ninduced $p$-norms of [[def-induced-matrix-p-norm]].\n\n1. **Compatibility.** For every $A\\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb{R})$ and every\n   $x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $$\\lVert Ax\\rVert_p\\;\\le\\;\\lVert A\\rVert_p\\,\\lVert x\\rVert_p.$$\n2. **Submultiplicativity.** For every $A\\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb{R})$ and\n   $B\\in M_{n\\times q}(\\mathbb{R})$,\n   $$\\lVert AB\\rVert_p\\;\\le\\;\\lVert A\\rVert_p\\,\\lVert B\\rVert_p.$$\n3. **Normalisation.** For every $n\\ge 1$,\n   $$\\lVert I_n\\rVert_p=1,$$\n   where $I_n$ is the identity matrix of [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]];\n   at $n=0$ the unique empty matrix $I_0\\in M_0(\\mathbb{R})$ has\n   $\\lVert I_0\\rVert_p=0$, the convention of [[def-induced-matrix-p-norm]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, the series $\\sum_{n\\ge0}x^n/\\iota(n!)$ converges absolutely. Its power-series radius is therefore $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $M=\\|A\\|_1$. For any $T>0$ and $|t|\\le T$, [F1] gives $\\|t^kA^k/k!\\|_1\\le (TM)^k/k!$. The majorant series converges by [F2], so the matrix series is absolutely and uniformly Cauchy on $[-T,T]$ and defines $e^{tA}$. Since $T$ was arbitrary, it converges locally uniformly.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Fix $c\\in\\mathbb R$ and choose $R>|c|$. For $|h|<R-|c|$ and $k\\ge1$, the finite factorization $(c+h)^k-c^k=h\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}(c+h)^{k-1-j}c^j$ bounds both the norm of the $k$th matrix-series difference quotient and the norm of the $k$th proposed derivative term by $M(MR)^{k-1}/(k-1)!$. The sum of these bounds converges by [F2], so both tails are uniformly small; each finite partial sum is a matrix polynomial whose difference quotient tends to its ordinary derivative. Taking first a sufficiently large partial sum and then $h\\to0$ proves differentiability and gives $\\frac{d}{dt}e^{tA}|_{t=c}=\\sum_{k\\ge1}A^kc^{k-1}/(k-1)!=Ae^{cA}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Every term in the derivative series commutes with $A$, so the same sum equals $e^{cA}A$. The $k=0$ term in the defining series gives $e^{0A}=I$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For commuting $A,B$, the double series for $e^Ae^B$ is absolutely convergent because its term norms are bounded by $\\|A\\|_1^m\\|B\\|_1^n/(m!n!)$, whose double sum is finite by [F2]. Regroup by $r=m+n$; commutation and the binomial formula give $\\sum_{m+n=r}A^mB^n/(m!n!)=(A+B)^r/r!$. Summing over $r$ proves $e^Ae^B=e^{A+B}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
