          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $\\mu=0$, [F1] gives $N=0$ almost surely and $K\\ge0$, so the tail is zero. Now suppose $\\mu>0$ and put $w_k=e^{-\\mu}\\mu^k/k!$ and $\\rho=\\mu/(K+2)$. The hypothesis gives $0<\\rho<1$, and [F1]–[F3] yield $w_{k+1}/w_k=\\mu/(k+1)\\le\\rho$ for every $k\\ge K+1$.",
