        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-scalar-poisson-count-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite parameter $\\lambda\\ge0$, the **Poisson count law**\n$\\operatorname{Pois}(\\lambda)$ is the probability measure on\n$\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$ whose masses are\n$$\\operatorname{Pois}(\\lambda)\\{k\\}=e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!},\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb N_0,$$\nwhen $\\lambda>0$, and is the point mass at $0$ when $\\lambda=0$. The\nnormalization and the moment and generating-function identities used below are\nproved in [[lem-poisson-count-law-normalization-and-moments]]. The separate\nextended convention for a Poisson point-process count on a Borel set of infinite\nintensity is that the count equals $+\\infty$ almost surely; it is not a finite\nPoisson probability law.",
          "uses": [
