            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-poisson-count-law-normalization-and-moments",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite $\\lambda\\ge0$, the masses in\n[[def-scalar-poisson-count-law]] sum to one. If $N\\sim\\operatorname{Pois}(\\lambda)$,\nthen\n$$\\mathbb E[N]=\\lambda,\\qquad \\mathbb E[N(N-1)]=\\lambda^2,\\qquad \\mathbb E[N^2]=\\lambda^2+\\lambda,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(N)=\\lambda,$$\nand $\\mathbb E[s^N]=\\exp(\\lambda(s-1))$ for $0\\le s\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-xd-finite-matrix-exponential-series",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a finite real square matrix $A$, define\n\n$$e^{tA}:=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{t^kA^k}{k!}.$$\n\nThis series converges absolutely and uniformly for $t$ in every bounded interval. It is differentiable with $\\frac{d}{dt}e^{tA}=Ae^{tA}=e^{tA}A$ and $e^{0A}=I$. If finite square matrices $A$ and $B$ commute, then $e^Ae^B=e^{A+B}$.",
