          "source": "thm-countable-state-reaction-networks-lyapunov-nonexplosion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and Dependent Choice. Let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^d$ be countable and let a finite-channel system be as in [[def-reaction-network-propensity-system-and-generator]]. For an initial state $x_0\\in E$, construct its minimal reaction process recursively: at state $x$, wait an exponential time with parameter $\\lambda(x)$, choose channel $j$ with probability $a_j(x)/\\lambda(x)$, and move to $x+\\nu_j$; if $\\lambda(x)=0$, remain at $x$ forever. Use an independent stream of these exponential and uniform random inputs, whose countable product law exists by Countable Choice and Dependent Choice.\n\nFor $V:E\\to[1,\\infty)$, call $V$ **proper** when $\\{x:V(x)\\le n\\}$ is finite for every integer $n\\ge1$. For this possibly unbounded $V$, define the finite local expression\n$$(QV)(x):=\\sum_{j:a_j(x)>0}a_j(x)\\bigl(V(x+\\nu_j)-V(x)\\bigr);$$\nthis does not assert that $V$ belongs to the bounded-function domain of the generator in [F1]. Suppose $QV\\le cV$ for a constant $c\\ge0$. Define $T_m$ as the time of the $m$th reaction event, with $T_m=\\infty$ if fewer than $m$ events occur. Then the explosion time $\\tau_\\infty:=\\lim_{m\\to\\infty}T_m$ satisfies $\\mathbb P_{x_0}(\\tau_\\infty=\\infty)=1$. No full Axiom of Choice is used.",
